  if it turns out smaller than it sounds.

  Context worth flagging before the next question: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is loaded as a bare [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m ([38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m) with
  no modules, no bundler, and no test runner in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. A shared cross-app module has to answer "how does it get
  loaded" too. I'll fold that into the design.

  Next question. [1mThe fork:[0m what the store actually holds decides whether this is a convenience cache or a
  security-relevant artifact.

  - [1mJust the identity[0m ([38;5;153muserId[39m, maybe a display name) — a cache of who's logged in. Wrong values cause a wrong greeting,
    not a breach. Cheap, no XSS exposure worth arguing about, and cheap to extend later.
  - [1mIdentity plus an auth token[0m — what you need if other forms will make authenticated requests. Costs you a real
    decision about storage: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is readable by any script on the page (XSS lifts the token), which is why
    token-bearing apps usually push the credential into an httpOnly cookie the JS never sees and keep only the identity
    client-side.
  - [1mA full session object[0m (identity, token, expiry, refresh) — needed if sessions expire and renew. Most expensive; hard
    to add halfway later because expiry handling touches every consumer.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m store identity only client-side and, when a real backend arrives, let the credential live in an
  httpOnly cookie. It gives the other forms what they need to display and attribute activity, and it keeps the token out
  of reach of page scripts. Expiry can be added later without redesign; token storage cannot easily be un-decided.
